  "final_text": "All four steps are done. The dashboard has two panels, the alert rule is created and enabled, and the response-code panel is retitled.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Logs' (index `kibana_sample_data_logs`) has fields including `timestamp`, `response`, `response.keyword`, `clientip`, `bytes`, `geo.dest`, and `url`.\n2. **Dashboard:** 'S4-9a5154a2 Web logs' (id `s4-9a5154a2-web-logs-ed93b450d518`) has two panels:\n   - \"Requests over time\": a count line chart over a date histogram on `timestamp`.\n   - \"Requests by response code\": a count bar chart split by `response.keyword`, top 10 terms. Its time range is the last 7 days.\n3. **Alert rule:** 'S4-9a5154a2 5xx errors' (id `45012707-14ac-4f29-8772-3e2c90209d8e`) is an `.es-query` rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`, checked every 1m. It fires when the count of documents with `response.keyword` from \"500\" to \"599\" is greater than 10 over a 5-minute window. I created it with `enabled: true`, so no separate enable call was needed.\n4. **Panel edit:** The response-code panel (index 1) is now titled \"Responses by code\". I re-read the dashboard and the title is there.\n\nThe rule read back as enabled, with status `ok`.\n\nOne assumption to check: `response` is stored as a string, so the 500\u2013599 filter is a lexicographic range on `response.keyword`. That is correct for three-digit codes, but a non-numeric value such as \"5xx\" would also fall in the range. The rule's query is not validated server-side, so I did not run it to confirm the match count.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
